with q as (
  select $l16_quiz$[{"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is the X-ray sky a 'map of violence' \u2014 a completely different population of objects than the calm optical sky?", "options": ["X-rays are just optical light in a different color, so it's the same objects", "By Wien's law, thermal X-ray emission requires MILLIONS of degrees \u2014 a temperature ordinary stars never reach, found only where matter falls into a compact object or is shock-heated by an explosion", "X-rays are emitted by all cool objects, so the X-ray sky shows ordinary stars", "The X-ray sky is empty; there is nothing violent to see"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A photon's energy is a receipt for the violence that made it: to impart thousands of eV to a single photon, the emitting process must be extreme. Wien's law (\u03bb_peak = b/T) makes this quantitative \u2014 the Sun's 5,800 K surface peaks in the visible (why we see it), but to shift the peak all the way down to X-ray wavelengths you need a temperature thousands of times hotter, i.e. MILLIONS of degrees. Ordinary stellar processes don't produce exposed, radiating million-degree gas. Such gas occurs only in catastrophic places: accretion onto neutron stars and black holes, supernova shock waves, magnetically confined plasma. So the X-ray sky isn't the optical sky recolored \u2014 it's a genuinely different set of objects (the compact, the collapsed, the exploding), selected by the requirement of extreme temperature.", "id": "q1"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Accreting neutron stars and black holes are the fiercest steady X-ray sources in the universe. Why do they far outshine (in X-rays) any star powered by fusion?", "options": ["They are simply much bigger than stars", "Accretion onto a compact object converts up to ~20% of the infalling matter's rest-mass energy to radiation \u2014 about 30\u00d7 more efficient than fusion's ~0.7% \u2014 because the gravitational well is so deep (energy scales as M/R, and they are the most compact objects)", "They contain more hydrogen to fuse", "Fusion actually releases more energy per kilogram than accretion"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "When gas spirals onto a compact object it heats by friction and compression to millions of degrees, converting gravitational potential energy to radiation. The energy released per unit mass is roughly GM/R, and as a fraction of mc\u00b2 that is ~0.03% for a white dwarf, ~20% for a neutron star, and up to ~40% for a black hole \u2014 versus nuclear fusion's ~0.7%. So neutron-star accretion is about 30\u00d7 more efficient than the fusion that lights the stars, which is why a compact object only kilometers across can outshine a whole galaxy of stars in X-rays. The key is COMPACTNESS: the yield scales as M/R, so the deepest wells (most mass in the smallest radius) release the most as matter falls in \u2014 which is why a white dwarf, far less compact, yields ~1,500\u00d7 less per kilogram than a neutron star.", "id": "q2"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "All X-ray and gamma-ray astronomy must be done from space \u2014 and this is categorically different from the optical case. Why?", "options": ["Space telescopes are always sharper, so all astronomy eventually moves to space", "The atmosphere completely ABSORBS X-rays and gamma rays (not one photon reaches the ground), so there is no degraded-but-possible ground option \u2014 unlike optical light, which the air transmits, so ground optical astronomy is merely less sharp", "X-rays travel too fast for ground telescopes to catch", "Ground telescopes are too small for X-rays"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "For optical light the atmosphere is a degrader: it transmits visible light but blurs it (seeing), so ground optical astronomy is possible, just less sharp \u2014 space is BETTER but not required. For X-rays and gamma rays the atmosphere is an absolute barrier: the air's molecules absorb every X-ray photon, and gamma rays are absorbed in particle cascades high up, so not a single cosmic X-ray reaches the ground. There is no high site, no window, no workaround \u2014 the telescope must be above essentially all the atmosphere. This is exactly why it took until 1962, when rockets could lift instruments above the air, to detect the first cosmic X-ray source: Giacconi's six minutes above the atmosphere were enough to open a field that had been completely invisible from the ground. Absorption forbids; seeing merely degrades.", "id": "q3"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why can't an ordinary telescope mirror focus X-rays, and what is the 'stone-skipping' solution?", "options": ["X-rays are too dim; the solution is a bigger mirror", "Struck head-on, an X-ray plows into the mirror and is absorbed (or passes through) rather than reflecting \u2014 but it WILL reflect off a surface struck at a shallow grazing angle, like a stone skipping on water, so X-ray mirrors are nested barrels off which X-rays graze to a focus", "X-rays reflect fine off ordinary mirrors; there is no problem", "The solution is to use lenses instead of mirrors"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "An X-ray photon is so energetic and short-wavelength that, striking a mirror head-on, it doesn't bounce \u2014 it plows into the material and knocks electrons loose (absorbed), or passes through. So normal reflection fails, and for a while X-ray IMAGING seemed impossible. The fix is grazing incidence: hit a polished surface within about a degree of edge-on and the X-ray reflects (total external reflection) \u2014 exactly like a stone thrown at a shallow angle skipping off water while one thrown straight down plunges in. This makes an X-ray 'mirror' a long, nearly-cylindrical barrel (a Wolter design, two grazing reflections), not a dish. A single barrel is a thin ring with tiny collecting area, so many shells are NESTED inside one another to gather enough of the scarce X-rays \u2014 Chandra uses four nested pairs to reach 0.5-arcsecond resolution, with mirrors polished to atomic smoothness because at grazing incidence the slightest roughness scatters the barely-reflecting X-rays.", "id": "q4"}, {"type": "mcq", "prompt": "Gamma-ray bursts were discovered by the Vela satellites \u2014 an accident with a notable irony. What is it, and what makes a GRB so extreme?", "options": ["The satellites were designed to find GRBs; they release about as much energy as a firecracker", "The satellites were built to detect the gamma-ray flashes of clandestine nuclear tests \u2014 and instead found far greater flashes coming from space; a GRB releases in seconds an energy comparable to the Sun's entire ten-billion-year lifetime output", "They were weather satellites; a GRB is a routine solar flare", "They were built to study the Moon; a GRB is a nearby stellar flare"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Vela satellites were launched in the late 1960s to watch for clandestine nuclear weapons tests, which produce gamma-ray flashes. They did detect flashes \u2014 but not from Earth; they came from random directions in space, lasting seconds. The irony: instruments built to catch the gamma rays of humanity's own weapons discovered the universe setting off vastly greater explosions of its own. A gamma-ray burst releases, in a few seconds, an energy comparable to what the Sun will emit over its entire ten-billion-year lifetime, from cosmological distances \u2014 so bright we see them across most of the observable universe. Their origins are the collapse of the most massive stars into black holes (long bursts) and neutron-star mergers (short bursts \u2014 one in 2017 seen simultaneously in gamma rays and gravitational waves). A GRB is a black hole being born. Gamma rays cannot be focused at all (they punch through any mirror), so they are caught by coded-aperture shadow masks and by tracking the particle showers they produce.", "id": "q5"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "Explain 'the impossible telescope': why an ordinary mirror cannot focus X-rays, the grazing-incidence solution and why X-ray telescopes are nested barrels, and why gamma rays cannot be focused at all \u2014 ending with the general principle linking photon energy to how hard the light is to catch.", "rubric": "Why an ordinary mirror fails: optical telescopes work because visible photons reflect off a mirror's surface, and curvature focuses them. An X-ray is so energetic and short-wavelength that, striking a surface HEAD-ON, it does not bounce \u2014 it plows into the material and is absorbed (knocking electrons loose) or passes straight through. To an X-ray a mirror is a slab to penetrate, not a reflective surface, so normal reflection (and thus normal focusing) fails completely; early X-ray astronomy could only DETECT X-rays and get rough directions, not image them. The grazing-incidence solution: X-rays WILL reflect if they strike at a very shallow, grazing angle (within ~1\u00b0 of the surface at 1 keV) \u2014 total external reflection \u2014 exactly like a stone thrown at a shallow angle skipping off water while one thrown straight down plunges in. The critical angle is tiny and shrinks with energy (roughly as 1/E: ~0.2\u00b0 at 5 keV, ~0.1\u00b0 at 10 keV). So an X-ray 'mirror' is not a dish but a long, nearly-cylindrical BARREL (sections of paraboloid and hyperboloid \u2014 the Wolter design, two grazing reflections in sequence) off which the X-rays skip to a focus. Why NESTED: a single barrel is a thin ring with a tiny collecting area, catching very few of the already-scarce X-rays, so many mirror shells are nested one inside another (concentric barrels), all focusing to the same point, to build up collecting area \u2014 Chandra uses four nested Wolter pairs to reach 0.5-arcsecond resolution, with mirrors among the smoothest surfaces ever made because at grazing incidence the slightest roughness scatters the barely-reflecting X-rays and ruins the image. Why gamma rays can't be focused at all: they are so penetrating that no mirror at any angle reflects them \u2014 they punch through even a grazing surface (a gamma ray passes through metres of lead) \u2014 so focusing optics are impossible. Instead: CODED-APERTURE MASKS (a patterned plate casts a shadow on a position-sensitive detector, shifted by arrival direction; analyze the shadow to reconstruct direction \u2014 a sophisticated pinhole camera, low-resolution but workable) and PARTICLE DETECTION (a gamma ray converts to a shower of electron-positron pairs, which are tracked to reconstruct the original \u2014 Fermi does this internally; ground-based Cherenkov telescopes watch the blue flash a gamma ray triggers at the top of the atmosphere, turning the atmosphere itself into part of the detector). The general principle: as photon energy climbs (optical \u2192 X-ray \u2192 gamma-ray) the light becomes progressively harder to focus \u2014 just reflect it (optical), skip it off a grazing barrel (X-ray), or give up focusing and use shadows and particle showers (gamma-ray). The higher the energy, the greater the violence, and the harder the light is to catch \u2014 a fitting difficulty, since the most extreme physics produces the light hardest to see.", "id": "q6"}, {"type": "open", "prompt": "The high-energy universe teaches a lesson about the relationship between perception and reality. Lay out that lesson: what was hidden and why, what it took to reveal it, and what it implies about opening new windows on the cosmos. Weave in why the sky is violent (Wien/accretion) and why it is a space-only science.", "rubric": "The hidden universe: for all of human history until 1962, we saw only the gentle face of the cosmos \u2014 the serene, unchanging starlight of the OPTICAL window, the one band the atmosphere leaves open \u2014 and built our entire intuitive picture of the universe on that calm view. It was profoundly incomplete: it omitted the black holes, the neutron stars, the accretion disks, the supernova shocks, and the gamma-ray bursts \u2014 the entire violent universe, arguably where the MOST interesting physics happens (gravity and matter pushed to their absolute limits). Why it was hidden: not because these objects are rare or distant, but because our eyes and our atmosphere are tuned to the wrong window. Two reasons the high-energy sky is violent: (1) WIEN'S LAW \u2014 high-energy light is a receipt for extreme physics; thermal X-ray emission requires MILLIONS of degrees (the Sun's 5,800 K peaks in the visible; to peak in the X-ray needs a temperature thousands of times hotter), which ordinary stars never reach. (2) ACCRETION onto compact objects \u2014 matter falling into the deep gravitational wells of neutron stars and black holes heats to millions of degrees and blazes in X-rays, converting up to ~20% of infalling rest-mass energy to radiation, about 30\u00d7 more efficient than fusion \u2014 so million-degree, X-ray-emitting matter is the signature of catastrophe (accretion, supernova shocks, relativistic jets). Why space-only: the atmosphere ABSORBS every X-ray and gamma-ray photon (unlike optical light, which it merely blurs), so there is no ground option at all \u2014 which is why it took rockets, in 1962, to see the X-ray sky, and why the whole field is inseparable from spaceflight. What it took: leaving the planet AND reinventing the telescope for light that refuses to be focused (grazing-incidence nested barrels for X-rays; shadow masks and particle-trackers for gamma rays). The lesson about perception and reality: entire universes of phenomena were invisible to all of human existence, and the only way to know they exist is to rise above the barrier and look. Every time we open a new window \u2014 X-ray, gamma-ray, and later gravitational waves and neutrinos \u2014 we find it crowded with things we never imagined, because the universe is always richer than the narrow band our biology happened to evolve to see. The serene night sky was the calmest possible view of a cosmos that is, in its high-energy light, a maelstrom \u2014 the universe was on fire, and we could not see the flames. A strong answer connects this to the deep reason we go to space to see: not merely for sharpness, but because some of reality is simply undetectable from the ground, and perception is not the same as reality.", "id": "q7"}]$l16_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
